course: dictionary
lesson: 7589
title: Pricey: a casual word for expensive
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Pricey is an informal word, the kind you hear in everyday talk and when shopping. It means expensive, so it costs more money than usual. You can say a bit pricey or too pricey, and the word changes to pricier and priciest.
explain: Look at the words around it. «The new bike was a bit pricey, so we waited for the sale.» A bit pricey means it costs more than we wanted to pay. Swap in expensive and the sentence still makes sense, so you know you have the right meaning.
explain: Casual words can make a cost sound smaller than it is. «A tad pricey» means a little expensive, but tad is casual too. When you hear a phrase like this, look at the actual numbers before you decide, because the numbers tell you the truth.
fact: Pricey is an adjective, and it is informal, which means it is a casual word for everyday talk.
fact: Pricey means expensive, costing a lot of money.
fact: The forms are pricey, pricier and priciest.
fact: «A bit pricey» and «too pricey» are common phrases.
fact: When someone says «a tad pricey», the casual words can make a real cost sound smaller, so check the price itself.
exercise: «The new bike was a bit pricey.» What does pricey mean here? | Slow to ride | Shiny and new | Cheap and small | *Expensive, costing a lot of money
exercise: Which word is the comparative form of pricey, meaning more expensive? | pricy | priciest | prices | *pricier
exercise: Mum says, «That coat is too pricey.» What does she mean? | It is too warm for summer | It is too small to wear | *It costs too much money | It is too bright to see
test: «The café is pricey, so we only ate there once.» Which word could replace pricey without changing the meaning? | Quiet | Empty | Cheap | *Expensive
test: Which word is the superlative of pricey, meaning the most expensive? | pricest | *priciest | pricer | pricey-most
exam: Ella says, «The tickets are a tad pricey, but the price is £5 - no wait, £4.» What is the real price? | Free | *£4 | £9 | £5
